    "narrative": "With the league\u2019s acute focus on stolen base efficiency, our lineup construction for today leans heavily into favorable SP matchups and park-aided upside. **Corbin Carroll** (77.56) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91) anchor the squad; **Corbin Carroll** remains an auto-start despite the presence of an elite hold pitcher, as his pedigree and the favorable park conditions mitigate the risk. Similarly, **Tyler Soderstrom** benefits from a substantial +16% park adjustment and a generous wind forecast, making him a high-floor play against Baz. **Trevor Story** (71.91) and **Josh Naylor** (71.44) provide excellent mid-to-high-tier stability, with the latter capitalizing on a matchup against the hittable Anthony Kay, whose high xERA (5.28) offers significant run-production potential.\n\nPlatoon advantages are driving our middle-infield selections, notably **Jose Caballero** (56.90), whose +13% dynamic platoon edge against Kyle Harrison keeps him ahead of the bench options despite the lower raw projection. The most difficult personnel decision today involved **Alex Freeland**, who sits on the bench with a 52.76 score. While he brings switch-hitting versatility, he was edged out by **Cole Young**\u2014whose 54.82 score is bolstered by a favorable wind-out projection and a matchup against a weak opposing starter, tipping the scales in **Cole Young**'s favor for the 2B slot. \n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding our \"Lineup Pending\" designations, as **Gabriel Moreno** and **Dalton Rushing** are currently carrying the bottom end of our projected output (58.61 and 58.70, respectively). While **Dalton Rushing** draws a tough assignment against Spencer Strider, the +17% SP skill adjustment keeps him viable. Users should watch for final lineup confirmations for **Brice Turang** and **Mickey Moniak**, as any shift downward in the batting order would necessitate a reassessment of their daily value, particularly given the reliance on these players to maintain our narrow edge in the SB category.",
